course: dictionary
lesson: 11894
title: Informal words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are informal. People use them in friendly, everyday talk, not in school essays or formal letters. Brolly is one of these words. It is a chatty word for umbrella, and you will hear it in Britain, Australia and New Zealand.
explain: Read the sentence: «Take your brolly, it's going to rain!» The words "it's going to rain" are the clue. You take something with you when it rains to keep dry, so a brolly must be something for the rain. That is an umbrella.
explain: Informal words are not wrong, but they fit best with friends and family. In a story or a formal letter, you would write umbrella instead. Knowing both words helps you choose the right one for where you are writing.
fact: Brolly is an informal noun, so you use it in relaxed, everyday talk.
fact: Brolly means an umbrella. It is used mainly in the UK, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: Brolly is short for umbrella. The plural is brollies: two brollies.
fact: «Take your brolly, it's going to rain!» Here brolly means umbrella.
exercise: «Take your brolly, it's going to rain!» What is a brolly? | *An umbrella | A bus ticket | A raincoat with a hood | A pair of wellies
exercise: «The sky is grey, so Mum packed two brollies for our trip.» How many brollies did Mum pack? | One | *Two | None | Three
exercise: Which word means the same as brolly but is more formal? | Brolly | Brill | Cuppa | *Umbrella
test: «Dad held his brolly over Mia's head so she stayed dry.» What does brolly mean here? | A walking stick | A sunny hat | A warm coat | *An umbrella
test: Which sentence uses the more formal word for a school report? | We took our hats. | We took our wellies. | *We took our umbrellas because it was raining. | We took our brollies, mate.
exam: Priya says, «Brolly and umbrella mean the same thing, so why do people say brolly?» Why would someone say brolly? | It is only used in Scotland | It is a formal word used in exams | It means a sunny day | *It is a friendly, informal word used in everyday talk
